# Text is LF in the repository and LF in the working copy, on every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout converts Go sources to CRLF, gofmt reports
# every file as unformatted, and `make lint` fails locally while the same
# commit is green in CI. A gate that means different things on different
# machines is not a gate.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Line endings are content here, not formatting.
#
# The golden vector sets carry a registered sha256 over their exact bytes
# (ADR-0002 2.7), and both the Go runner and the Python cross-check verify that
# digest before reading a set. A checkout that converted LF to CRLF would change
# every digest and fail the corpus on one platform and not the other, which is
# exactly the class of divergence the corpus exists to catch.
testdata/golden/** text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else whose bytes are hashed or signed as it lands.
*.json text eol=lf

# Review noise, not content. GitHub collapses these in a pull request's diff
# and leaves them out of the language statistics; they are still diffed,
# committed and gated exactly as before.
#
# vendor/ is upstream's code, committed on purpose (ADR-0001 control 6) and
# verified by `make vendor-verify` rather than by reading it. The rest is
# generated from the contract or the register, and CI fails if any of it
# differs from what its generator produces — so the thing to review is the
# source it came from, which stays expanded.
backend/vendor/** linguist-vendored linguist-generated
backend/internal/transport/http/gen/types.gen.go linguist-generated
backend/postman/ZoikoTax.postman_collection.json* linguist-generated
contracts/openapi/export/** linguist-generated
sdk/**/types.gen.go linguist-generated
